{"abstract":"Large Language Model (LLM) services and models often come with legal rules on *who* can use them and *how* they must use them. Assessing the compliance of the released LLMs is crucial, as these rules protect the interests of the LLM contributor and prevent misuse. In this context, we describe the novel fingerprinting problem of Black-box Identity Verification (BBIV). The goal is to determine whether a third-party application uses a certain LLM through its chat function. We propose a method called Targeted Random Adversarial Prompt (TRAP) that identifies the specific LLM in use. We repurpose adversarial suffixes, originally proposed for jailbreaking, to get a pre-defined answer from the target LLM, while other models give random answers. TRAP detects the target LLMs with over 95% true positive rate at under 0.2% false positive rate even after a single interaction. TRAP remains effective even if the LLM has minor changes that do not significantly alter the original function.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Gubri, Martin","Ulmer, Dennis Thomas","Lee, Hwaran","Yun, Sangdoo","Oh, Seong Joon"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-759402"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","South Korea"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Eberhard Karls University of Tübingen","Naver AI Lab","Parameter Lab","Tübingen AI Center"],"keyword":["Fingerprinting","jailbreaking"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"trap targeted random adversarial prompt honeypot for black box identification","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.12991","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-759402","ids":["itu-5eb3f726-0a16-49a7-8aae-87e4f66d3aaa"],"keyword":["adversarial prompts","black-box identity verification","fingerprinting","jailbreaking","model attribution"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-FF28B63BBA8B6126F73B745F86610441"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.683.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.12991","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"parameter lab","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Parameter Lab","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"kr","key":"naver ai lab","loc":["South Korea","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"Naver AI Lab","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"eberhard karls university of tübingen","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Eberhard Karls University of Tübingen","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"de","key":"tübingen ai center","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Tübingen AI Center","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"gubripau","last":"Gubri","name":"Gubri, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dennis Thomas","key":"ulmerpau","last":"Ulmer","locid":"6596de1d-ea29-4252-a22e-f98cf28090c6","name":"Ulmer, Dennis Thomas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Hwaran","key":"leepau","last":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hwaran","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Sangdoo","key":"yunpau","last":"Yun","name":"Yun, Sangdoo","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 4 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Seong Joon","key":"ohpau","last":"Oh","name":"Oh, Seong Joon","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lun-Wei","key":"kupedh","last":"Ku","name":"Ku, Lun-Wei","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Andre","key":"martinspedh","last":"Martins","name":"Martins, Andre","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Vivek","key":"srikumarpedh","last":"Srikumar","name":"Srikumar, Vivek","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2402.12991","pages":"11496-11517","place":"Bangkok","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","title":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics ACL 2024","volume":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics ACL 2024"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5eb3f726-0a16-49a7-8aae-87e4f66d3aaa"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"TRAP: Targeted Random Adversarial Prompt Honeypot for Black-Box Identification","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-759402","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The last decade in deep learning has brought on increasingly capable systems that are deployed on a wide variety of applications. In natural language processing, the field has been transformed by a number of breakthroughs including large language models, which are used in increasingly many user-facing applications. In order to reap the benefits of this technology and reduce potential harms, it is important to quantify the reliability of model predictions and the uncertainties that shroud their development. This thesis studies how uncertainty in natural language processing can be characterized from a linguistic, statistical and neural perspective, and how it can be reduced and quantified through the design of the experimental pipeline. We further explore uncertainty quantification in modeling by theoretically and empirically investigating the effect of inductive model biases in text classification tasks. The corresponding experiments include data for three different languages (Danish, English and Finnish) and tasks as well as a large set of different uncertainty quantification approaches. Additionally, we propose a method for calibrated sampling in natural language generation based on non-exchangeable conformal prediction, which provides tighter token sets with better coverage of the actual continuation. Lastly, we develop an approach to quantify confidence in large black-box language models using auxiliary predictors, where the confidence is predicted from the input to and generated output text of the target model alone.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Ulmer, Dennis Thomas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776390"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on uncertainty in natural language processing","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109068949/thesis_final_print.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776390","ids":["itu-3c0d7bac-d02e-4684-9e93-5f6bf698315b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-7F7C5685DA583C986D0C80E4847491B6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109068949/thesis_final_print.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dennis Thomas","key":"ulmerpau","last":"Ulmer","locid":"6596de1d-ea29-4252-a22e-f98cf28090c6","name":"Ulmer, Dennis Thomas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779495241"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"227","pages":"364","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3c0d7bac-d02e-4684-9e93-5f6bf698315b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On Uncertainty In Natural Language Processing","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776390","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Split conformal prediction has recently sparked great interest due to its ability to provide formally guaranteed uncertainty sets or intervals for predictions made by black-box neural models, ensuring a predefined probability of containing the actual ground truth. While the original formulation assumes data exchangeability, some extensions handle non-exchangeable data, which is often the case in many real-world scenarios. In parallel, some progress has been made in conformal methods that provide statistical guarantees for a broader range of objectives, such as bounding the best F1-score or minimizing the false negative rate in expectation. In this paper, we leverage and extend these two lines of work by proposing non-exchangeable conformal risk control, which allows controlling the expected value of any monotone loss function when the data is not exchangeable. Our framework is flexible, makes very few assumptions, and allows weighting the data based on its relevance for a given test example; a careful choice of weights may result on tighter bounds, making our framework useful in the presence of change points, time series, or other forms of distribution drift. Experiments with both synthetic and real world data show the usefulness of our method.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Farinhas, António","Zerva, Chrysoula","Ulmer, Dennis Thomas","Martins, André"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-747234"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Portugal"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Instituto de Telecomunicações","University of Lisbon"],"keyword":["Conformal Prediction","Uncertainty","conformal risk control","non exchangeable data"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"non exchangeable conformal risk control","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=j511LaqEeP","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-747234","ids":["itu-7117ba3f-0761-4978-808e-cbfd687e04e2"],"keyword":["conformal prediction","conformal risk control","non-exchangeable data","uncertainty"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-BE637400088523906867E0CBF8444C06"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=j511LaqEeP","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.01262","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"pt","key":"university of lisbon","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Lisbon","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"pt","key":"instituto de telecomunicações","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Instituto de Telecomunicações","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"António","key":"farinhaspau","last":"Farinhas","name":"Farinhas, António","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Chrysoula","key":"zervapau","last":"Zerva","name":"Zerva, Chrysoula","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Dennis Thomas","key":"ulmerpau","last":"Ulmer","locid":"6596de1d-ea29-4252-a22e-f98cf28090c6","name":"Ulmer, Dennis Thomas","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"André","key":"martinspau","last":"Martins","name":"Martins, André","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"edition":"2024","status":"p","title":"Twelfth International Conference on Learning Representations","volume":"ICLR"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7117ba3f-0761-4978-808e-cbfd687e04e2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Non-exchangeable conformal risk control","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-747234","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Large language models (LLMs) are powerful dialogue agents, but specializing them towards fulfilling a specific function can be challenging. Instructing tuning, i.e. tuning models on instruction and sample responses generated by humans (Ouyang et al., 2022), has proven as an effective method to do so, yet requires a number of data samples that a) might not be available or b) costly to generate. Furthermore, this cost increases when the goal is to make the LLM follow a specific workflow within a dialogue instead of single instructions. Inspired by the self-play technique in reinforcement learning and the use of LLMs to simulate human agents, we propose a more effective method for data collection through LLMs engaging in a conversation in various roles. This approach generates a training data via \"self-talk\" of LLMs that can be refined and utilized for supervised fine-tuning. We introduce an automated way to measure the (partial) success of a dialogue. This metric is used to filter the generated conversational data that is fed back in LLM for training. Based on our automated and human evaluations of conversation quality, we demonstrate that such self-talk data improves results. In addition, we examine the various characteristics that showcase the quality of generated dialogues and how they can be connected to their potential utility as training data.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Ulmer, Dennis Thomas","Mansimov, Elman","Lin, Kaixiang","Sun, Justin","Gao, Xibin","Zhang, Yi"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-759413"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Amazon Web Services AI Labs"],"keyword":["Large Language Models"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bootstrapping llm based task oriented dialogue agents via self talk","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.05033","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-759413","ids":["itu-a6e2dd9e-6851-42bf-9d0a-52f7e84d87b1"],"keyword":["Automated dialogue evaluation","Instruction tuning","Large language models","Self-talk data","Supervised fine-tuning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-C8C5416A3EE68217F57AF32298177F85"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.05033","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.05033","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"amazon web services ai labs","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Amazon Web Services AI Labs","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dennis Thomas","key":"ulmerpau","last":"Ulmer","locid":"6596de1d-ea29-4252-a22e-f98cf28090c6","name":"Ulmer, Dennis Thomas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Elman","key":"mansimovpau","last":"Mansimov","name":"Mansimov, Elman","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kaixiang","key":"linpau","last":"Lin","name":"Lin, Kaixiang","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Justin","key":"sunpau","last":"Sun","name":"Sun, Justin","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Xibin","key":"gaopau","last":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xibin","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Yi","key":"zhangpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lun-Wei","key":"kupedh","last":"Ku","name":"Ku, Lun-Wei","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Andre","key":"martinspedh","last":"Martins","name":"Martins, Andre","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Vivek","key":"srikumarpedh","last":"Srikumar","name":"Srikumar, Vivek","rank":9,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2401.05033","pages":"9500-9522","place":"Bangkok","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","title":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics ACL 2024","volume":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics ACL 2024"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a6e2dd9e-6851-42bf-9d0a-52f7e84d87b1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Bootstrapping LLM-based Task-Oriented Dialogue Agents via Self-Talk","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-759413","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Quantifying uncertainty in automatically generated text is important for letting humans check potential hallucinations and making systems more reliable. Conformal prediction is an attractive framework to provide predictions imbued with statistical guarantees, however, its application to text generation is challenging since any i.i.d. assumptions are not realistic. In this paper, we bridge this gap by leveraging recent results on *non-exchangeable* conformal prediction, which still ensures bounds on coverage. The result, *non-exchangeable conformal nucleus sampling*, is a novel extension of the conformal prediction framework to generation based on nearest neighbors. Our method can be used post-hoc for an arbitrary model without extra training and supplies token-level, calibrated prediction sets equipped with statistical guarantees. Experiments in machine translation and language modeling show encouraging results in generation quality. By also producing tighter prediction sets with good coverage, we thus give a more theoretically principled way to perform sampling with conformal guarantees.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Ulmer, Dennis Thomas","Zerva, Chrysoula","Martins, André"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-747248"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Portugal"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Instituto de Telecomunicações","University of Lisbon"],"keyword":["Conformal Prediction","Non Exchangeable Conformal Nucleus Sampling","Statistical Guarantees","Text Generation","Uncertainty quantification"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"non exchangeable conformal language generation with nearest neighbors","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-eacl.129.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-747248","identifier":[{"id":"85188720169","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8de7a07d-a214-43db-b1ae-46a0e4a06587"],"keyword":["Conformal Prediction","Non-Exchangeable Conformal Nucleus Sampling","Statistical Guarantees","Text Generation","Uncertainty Quantification"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-068F12B8AB56993935C1785674EA63BE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-eacl.129.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"pt","key":"instituto de telecomunicações","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Instituto de Telecomunicações","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"pt","key":"university of lisbon","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Lisbon","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dennis Thomas","key":"ulmerpau","last":"Ulmer","locid":"6596de1d-ea29-4252-a22e-f98cf28090c6","name":"Ulmer, Dennis Thomas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Chrysoula","key":"zervapau","last":"Zerva","name":"Zerva, Chrysoula","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"André","key":"martinspau","last":"Martins","name":"Martins, André","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Yvette","key":"grahampedh","last":"Graham","name":"Graham, Yvette","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Matthew","key":"purverpedh","last":"Purver","name":"Purver, Matthew","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"edition":"2024","pages":"1909-1929","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","title":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EACL 2024","volume":"EACL"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8de7a07d-a214-43db-b1ae-46a0e4a06587"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Non-Exchangeable Conformal Language Generation with Nearest Neighbors","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-747248","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
